WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:05.400
From filming inside of a dormant volcano to&nbsp;
insurance policies that covered kidnapping,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.120
here are some lesser known&nbsp;
facts about 1999's "The Mummy".

00:00:09.660 --> 00:00:14.820
The fictional Rick O'Connell and Evelyn Carnahan&nbsp;
certainly deal with more than their share of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.820 --> 00:00:20.280
plagues and curses in "The Mummy." The crew and&nbsp;
cast of actors including stars Brendan Fraser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:25.560
and Rachel Weisz, also dealt with a few plagues&nbsp;
of their own making the film. Filming out in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:25.560 --> 00:00:31.020
desert was no easy task when temperatures often&nbsp;
hit 130 degrees Fahrenheit each morning. Not to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:31.020 --> 00:00:36.627
mention, the Moroccan dunes were a cozy home&nbsp;
to a host of creeping, slithering creatures.

00:00:36.627 --> 00:00:39.300
"What do you mean bugs? I hate bugs!"

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:44.340
A professional scorpion catcher was even stationed&nbsp;
on the set. Rachel Weisz told The Birmingham Post:

00:00:55.501 --> 00:00:59.663
"What is a place like me&nbsp;
doing in a girl like this?"

00:00:59.663 --> 00:01:01.260
In an interview with Entertainment Weekly,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.260 --> 00:01:05.580
Brendan Fraser talked about a particularly&nbsp;
memorable encounter with a poisonous snake:

00:01:26.880 --> 00:01:30.540
Sure, "The Mummy" was a fantastical&nbsp;
action-adventure blockbuster movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.540 --> 00:01:35.040
but there were grains of historic truth amid&nbsp;
all that sand. When director Sommers wasn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:35.040 --> 00:01:40.380
busy animating corpses, he called in the help&nbsp;
of noted Egyptologist Doctor Stuart Smith from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:40.380 --> 00:01:45.240
UCLA. Since no one today was around over two&nbsp;
thousand years ago to hear how people spoke&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:45.240 --> 00:01:50.580
ancient Egyptian. So Mummy movie star Arnold&nbsp;
Vosloo, who played the villainous Imhotep,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.580 --> 00:01:54.060
worked with Doctor Smith to get the dialogue&nbsp;
and the lines as accurate as possible.

00:01:54.060 --> 00:01:56.400
Sommers told Cinefantastique:

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.220
Vosloo was so committed to making his&nbsp;
character seem realistic and plausible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:02.220 --> 00:02:06.720
he worked for hours into the night, learning lines&nbsp;
phonetically over the phone with Doctor Smith.

00:02:06.937 --> 00:02:11.413
"Do-re-mi-fa-so-la-te-doooo!&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.100
I am in excellent voice today!"

00:02:14.100 --> 00:02:19.440
The crew was just as committed to creating as many&nbsp;
authentic props and sets as possible. Production&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.440 --> 00:02:24.026
designer Allan Cameron made long trips to&nbsp;
the British Museum to find his inspiration.

00:02:24.026 --> 00:02:24.923
Cameron said:

00:02:41.280 --> 00:02:46.020
Even though "The Mummy" is set in Egypt, filming&nbsp;
for the movie was done in Morocco and London.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:46.020 --> 00:02:50.940
The Sahara desert, near the city of Erfoud,&nbsp;
became the background for the desert scenes.

00:02:50.940 --> 00:02:56.520
The city of Marrakesh became an excellent stand-in&nbsp;
for 1920s Cairo as it had preserved much of its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.520 --> 00:03:00.900
traditional architecture. The film crew took down&nbsp;
telephone poles and electrical cables to make the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:00.900 --> 00:03:06.060
location appear even more like 1920's Egypt. The&nbsp;
town hall became the Cairo Museum of Antiquities,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:06.060 --> 00:03:10.500
which Evelyn promptly destroys in the film.&nbsp;
Marrakesh wasn't the only stand-in for another&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:14.940
city. Those memorable river scenes in "The&nbsp;
Mummy" were filmed on England's Thames River,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.940 --> 00:03:17.809
and not the Nile. So when Rick shouts out to Beni:

00:03:17.809 --> 00:03:23.490
"Hey Beni! Looks to me like you're&nbsp;
on the wrong side of the river!"

00:03:23.490 --> 00:03:26.370
He's being more accurate than many might realize!

00:03:26.700 --> 00:03:31.260
While it seems impossible now to imagine "The&nbsp;
Mummy" as anything other than a comedy-horror&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.260 --> 00:03:35.160
"hokum" flick, that's not how the film was&nbsp;
initially planned. "The Mummy" went through&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:35.160 --> 00:03:39.900
over nine years of development, multiple scripts,&nbsp;
and several directors before being turned over to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:39.900 --> 00:03:45.060
Stephen Sommers. Previous screen treatments were&nbsp;
contributed by "Innerspace's" Joe Dante, "Night of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:49.980
the Living Dead's" George Romero, "Hellraiser's"&nbsp;
Clive Barker, and "Shock Wave's'" Alan Ormsby.

00:03:49.980 --> 00:03:53.460
The earlier scripts were generally&nbsp;
more straightforward as horror films,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:53.460 --> 00:03:58.080
though one script set the mummy in Beverly&nbsp;
Hills, wreaking havoc at a local lab. There&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:58.080 --> 00:04:01.860
was even a discussion about having "The Mummy"&nbsp;
be more like "The Terminator." Tom Cruise,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.860 --> 00:04:06.180
Ben Affleck, and other popular male leads at&nbsp;
the time were rumored to be originally offered&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.180 --> 00:04:10.638
the part of Rick O'Connell. None of this&nbsp;
gossip is confirmed, no matter how juicy.

00:04:10.638 --> 00:04:13.457
"He's still… he's still…"

00:04:13.457 --> 00:04:14.184
"Juicy."

00:04:14.184 --> 00:04:18.785
Stephen Sommers was a childhood fan of&nbsp;
the original 1932 Boris Karloff film,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.785 --> 00:04:22.200
but that classic doesn't share much&nbsp;
with the modern remake. For starters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:22.200 --> 00:04:26.760
no one is confronted by a giant wall of sand.&nbsp;
Movie-goers in the late '90s were desperate for an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.760 --> 00:04:31.680
action hero that didn't take himself too seriously&nbsp;
and a mummy that embodied a real sense of danger.

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:35.067
Stephen Sommers explained it&nbsp;
this way to Cinefantastique:

00:04:44.377 --> 00:04:49.740
Stephen Sommers told Cinefantastique one of the&nbsp;
reasons Brendan Fraser was cast as Rick O'Connell:

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.580
"Oh yeah, this just keeps&nbsp;
gettin' better and better."

00:05:02.580 --> 00:05:06.720
For the scene in which Rick faces the&nbsp;
hangman's noose, Fraser was only needed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.720 --> 00:05:11.100
for close-ups. But in volunteering to do more for&nbsp;
the scene, he tells of a complete blacking out.

00:05:11.100 --> 00:05:14.220
As Fraser recently recalled the&nbsp;
incident for Entertainment Weekly:

00:05:29.160 --> 00:05:33.180
Sommers disputes this version of events,&nbsp;
saying that Fraser "is totally to blame."

00:05:33.180 --> 00:05:34.380
"I wouldn't say that."

00:05:34.380 --> 00:05:38.100
Nevertheless, the two continue to be&nbsp;
good friends. But the fact remains that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.100 --> 00:05:41.880
filming was intense. Fraser needed&nbsp;
multiple surgeries — including back&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:41.880 --> 00:05:45.900
surgery — after filming the first three&nbsp;
films due to injuries and physical strain.

00:05:46.500 --> 00:05:52.020
"I am a LIBRARIAN!"

00:05:52.020 --> 00:05:56.760
In "The Mummy," Rachel Weisz proved there was&nbsp;
more to a librarian than meets the eye. She did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:56.760 --> 00:06:01.047
some of her own stunts, including camel riding,&nbsp;
which despite what her character says on camera…

00:06:01.047 --> 00:06:03.468
"I think they're adorable!"

00:06:03.468 --> 00:06:07.440
Weisz didn't find them quite so&nbsp;
adorable off-camera. As for stunts,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:07.440 --> 00:06:09.540
one that concerned her was not her own,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.540 --> 00:06:13.440
but one that injured a stuntman during a&nbsp;
gunfight. As she told The Birmingham Post:

00:06:23.580 --> 00:06:26.040
Before her role as Evelyn Carnahan in "The Mummy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.040 --> 00:06:29.760
Weisz was still relatively unknown. She&nbsp;
confessed that she had not thought about&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:33.000
doing a big-budget movie before until&nbsp;
she saw the script for "The Mummy."

00:06:33.000 --> 00:06:34.560
As she explained to The Mirror:

00:06:38.580 --> 00:06:42.540
Oddly enough, after "The Mummy" was a&nbsp;
hit, Playboy invited Rachel Weisz to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:42.540 --> 00:06:46.200
model as her librarian character&nbsp;
for the magazine. She declined.

00:06:46.920 --> 00:06:50.520
The real-life Imhotep was not a&nbsp;
supervillain. He was a high priest,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:50.520 --> 00:06:54.960
an architect, and a physician. Imhotep&nbsp;
designed the Step Pyramid of Djoser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:54.960 --> 00:07:00.000
which is located in Saqqarah. The city of the&nbsp;
dead Hamanaptra, however, is purely fictional.

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:04.860
The fictional version of Imhotep appeared in&nbsp;
Universal's classic 1932 version of "The Mummy,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:04.860 --> 00:07:09.840
Boris Karloff also portrays Imhotep searching&nbsp;
for a modern-day reincarnation of his lost love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.840 --> 00:07:15.360
Ankh-esen-amun. It appears that the original&nbsp;
film's writers, John L. Balderston, Nina Wilcox&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:15.360 --> 00:07:20.580
Putnam, and Richard Schayer relied on ancient&nbsp;
Egyptian mythology and folklore in their work.

00:07:22.486 --> 00:07:24.600
Arnold Vosloo is a classically&nbsp;
trained South African actor and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.600 --> 00:07:27.240
horror fan who appeared in Ridley Scott's "1492:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:27.240 --> 00:07:32.040
Conquest of Paradise." Later he played the&nbsp;
lead in two direct-to-video "Darkman" films.

00:07:32.040 --> 00:07:34.920
Stephen Sommers recalled&nbsp;
Vosloo's audition for the Mummy:
 &nbsp;

00:07:47.100 --> 00:07:50.220
Vosloo intended to play Imhotep seriously with no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.220 --> 00:07:53.940
hint of silliness or camp. He wanted&nbsp;
to emphasize the character's romance.

00:07:53.940 --> 00:07:56.100
Vosloo told Cinefantastique:

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:06.300
In addition to examining his character's&nbsp;
motivations, Vosloo researched his physical&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.300 --> 00:08:09.960
appearance, including the fact that&nbsp;
Egyptian priests were fully shaven.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.640
Director Stephen Sommers agreed to try it&nbsp;
out and the results spoke for themselves.

00:08:14.640 --> 00:08:20.880
Kevin J. O'Connor played the hilariously evil Beni&nbsp;
Gabor, the opportunistic henchman for the mummy.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:20.880 --> 00:08:24.780
He had worked with director Stephen Sommers&nbsp;
before, on the action film, "Deep Rising."

00:08:24.780 --> 00:08:29.460
O'Connor created many aspects of his character.&nbsp;
He told interviewer Jim Conlan Chats that he&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:29.460 --> 00:08:33.420
asked the costuming department for pants&nbsp;
that didn't fit. He regretted requesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:33.420 --> 00:08:37.860
open-toed sandals after a wriggling creature&nbsp;
squiggled under the sand on the first day.

00:08:37.860 --> 00:08:42.840
One of O'Connor's most popular lines was inspired&nbsp;
by the giant sand wall effect, which none of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.840 --> 00:08:47.100
cast had seen yet. Based on a description,&nbsp;
O'Connor came up with this memorable ad-lib:

00:08:47.100 --> 00:08:50.520
"I loved the whole sand wall&nbsp;
trick. It was beautiful."

00:08:50.520 --> 00:08:53.760
The actor intentionally added the&nbsp;
line to reinforce the impression&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:53.760 --> 00:08:57.127
of a slimy employee man "kissing up to his boss."

00:08:57.127 --> 00:08:58.920
"You see me as God, right, Smithers?"

00:08:58.920 --> 00:09:00.240
"Absolutely, sir."

00:09:00.240 --> 00:09:01.620
"You'd kneel before me?"

00:09:01.620 --> 00:09:03.120
"Boy, would I!"

00:09:03.120 --> 00:09:07.140
Beni became a popular character, and even&nbsp;
though he was chewed up by beetles in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.140 --> 00:09:11.820
first film. Sommers talked with O'Connor about the&nbsp;
possibility of bringing Beni back for the sequels,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:11.820 --> 00:09:16.800
reanimated as a "discombobulated mummy."&nbsp;
In the end, with so much jammed into "The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.800 --> 00:09:20.827
Mummy Returns" there was no room for Beni&nbsp;
to return to his former spinelessness.

00:09:21.240 --> 00:09:26.160
A lot has been learned since Hollywood released&nbsp;
"The Mummy" in 1999. Like Imhotep himself,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:26.160 --> 00:09:29.820
there are aspects of his movie that don't&nbsp;
come across as very sensitive in today's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:29.820 --> 00:09:34.320
world. The film remains a beloved favorite&nbsp;
for many audiences, but in retrospect,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:34.320 --> 00:09:38.820
there appear to be a few bugs in it due to&nbsp;
its portrayals of ancient Egyptian culture&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:38.820 --> 00:09:43.440
and people of color. Since the movie's release,&nbsp;
several actors have rethought their casting.

00:09:43.440 --> 00:09:45.540
Arnold Vosloo told Entertainment Weekly:

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:57.360
Omid Djalili had ideas about playing his role of&nbsp;
Warden Gad Hassan from the beginning. In an effort&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.360 --> 00:10:01.740
to avoid offending audiences, he wondered if he&nbsp;
could play his role in a lighthearted manner even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:01.740 --> 00:10:06.060
though that wasn't in the script. He discussed&nbsp;
this with Stephen Sommers during casting.

00:10:06.060 --> 00:10:07.560
In an interview he explained:

00:10:33.840 --> 00:10:36.540
For this film, they found&nbsp;
a real volcano – at least&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:36.540 --> 00:10:40.020
a dormant one. Production designer&nbsp;
Allan Cameron was up to the task,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:40.020 --> 00:10:44.820
flying to Morocco and heading out to the dunes&nbsp;
of Erfoud where he discovered the volcano.

00:10:44.820 --> 00:10:46.800
As Stephen Sommers told Film Scouts:

00:10:57.300 --> 00:11:01.140
Over the course of 16 weeks, the crew&nbsp;
built the city of Hamunaptra out in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:01.140 --> 00:11:04.800
the desert. These sets were designed to&nbsp;
collapse during the final action scenes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.800 --> 00:11:07.980
Interior scenes were filmed in&nbsp;
London's Shepperton Studios.

00:11:07.980 --> 00:11:10.860
Imagine being responsible for&nbsp;
the housing, feeding, health,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.860 --> 00:11:15.840
and safety of over 800 actors, extras, crew&nbsp;
members, and even Tuareg horse riders in the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:15.840 --> 00:11:20.100
middle of the desert! And what if the city&nbsp;
wasn't used to handling 800 visitors at once?

00:11:20.100 --> 00:11:22.200
As co-producer Patricia Carr explained:

00:11:29.160 --> 00:11:34.680
During location filming, producer James, aka&nbsp;
"Jim" Jacks actually took out insurance policies&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:34.680 --> 00:11:39.480
for kidnapping. According to Brendan&nbsp;
Fraser, the policies were each worth …

00:11:39.480 --> 00:11:41.640
"One Million Dollars!"

00:11:42.600 --> 00:11:47.460
While that's not exactly loose change, Fraser&nbsp;
expressed some concern over the figure, saying:

00:11:52.080 --> 00:11:54.120
"I only gamble with my life, never my money."

00:11:54.120 --> 00:11:58.260
Not all the stars had the same insurance.&nbsp;
The insurance coverage for Kevin J. O'Connor,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:58.260 --> 00:12:02.100
who played Beni, was for only $50,000.&nbsp;
He wasn't too bothered about it, though.

00:12:02.100 --> 00:12:04.020
He joked to producer Jim Jacks:

00:12:06.180 --> 00:12:10.140
With a parade of sequels, video games,&nbsp;
a prequel, and an animated series,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:10.140 --> 00:12:15.360
"The Mummy" became the last great action-adventure&nbsp;
franchise before the superhero craze. All things&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.360 --> 00:12:18.540
eventually run their course, but you never&nbsp;
know when this mummy will rise again.

